WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:46.170
Charles

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.260
De Gaulle was the sort of man who turned heads.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.899
At six foot five inches in height, he towered
over his contemporaries, and, with his regal

00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:59.440
bearing and firm gaze, he exuded an air of
forthright self-confidence.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.610
Throughout his life, his unswerving love for
France, coupled with his determination to

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:08.570
right the wrongs of the past, turned him into
a hero of the resistance movement and one

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.420
of the French Republic’s truly great statesmen.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:17.820
In Today’s Biographics, we examine the very
full life of Charles De Gaulle,

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.429
Early Life

00:01:22.429 --> 00:01:27.440
Charles Andre Joseph Marie De Gaulle was born
on November 22, 1890.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.100
He was the third of five children to Henri
and Jean De Gaulle.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.400
Henri was a military man who had served in
the Franco-Prussian War.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.739
By the time his second son was born he was
a teacher at a Catholic school in the French

00:01:38.739 --> 00:01:40.660
industrial region of Lille.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:45.390
Shortly after the birth of Charles, the family
moved to Paris, where Henri took up a position

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:49.050
as the headmaster of the Jesuit College of
the Immaculate Conception.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.400
Henri was a lover of history and the glory
of France.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.459
Nights in the De Gaulle home would often be
spent with the children gathered around to

00:01:56.459 --> 00:02:01.360
listen to their father regale them with stories
of the country’s history and the part their

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:02.840
ancestors had played in it.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.920
Charles would later recall the stories his
father would tell about their most famous

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.421
ancestor, Sieur Jehan De Gaulle, a knight
who fought against the English at the Battle

00:02:11.421 --> 00:02:12.610
of Agincourt.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.410
The glorious history of his nation that Charles
was learning at the feet of his father came

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.410
with a distrust of the country’s natural
enemy, the British.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:23.480
Henri would not allow his children to learn
the English language.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.630
Charles’ mother, Jean, loved her country
even more than her husband.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:30.900
Charles would later say that she had . . .
An uncompromising love for her country that

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.610
was equal to her religious piety.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:38.390
The defeat of France at the battle of Sedan
in 1870 during the Franco-Prussian war was

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:42.560
a source of humiliation for all French patriots,
including the De Gaulle’s.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.410
They seethed with the desire to avenge the
shame that had fallen upon their once mighty

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:48.410
nation.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:52.250
They kept this ambition alive in their children,
with Henri regularly taking them to the cemetery

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:57.730
at Le Bourget and having them read aloud the
inscription to the dead of 1870 . . .

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:03.280
The sword of France, broken in their valiant
hands, will once more be reformed by their

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.280
descendants.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.600
Even though he wasn’t the smartest of the
five De Gaulle children, Charles was their

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:08.600
natural leader.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.890
He had a mischievous, dominant attitude and
he was always organizing some sort of practical

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.650
joke on one of his siblings.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:19.750
When he was about six, he invented his own
language, which involved speaking French backwards.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.069
He forced his brothers and sisters to learn
it.

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:26.780
They naturally objected but his insistence
paid off and they all became fluent at it.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.459
This was an early sign of the determination
and persistent force of character that would

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:36.670
come to be his trademark as an adult.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:42.710
A Model Soldier
Like most French boys, Charles loved to play

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:43.710
soldiers.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.900
Yet, when he and his brothers lined up their
model armies on the bedroom floor, he would

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:51.410
exhibit a seriousness and intensity that went
way beyond a childhood game.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:55.680
He always insisted on portraying the French
army and would become cold and dispassionate

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.200
during the war games as he put his total energies
into defeating the enemy.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.610
Charles attended the Jesuit College where
his father was the headmaster.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:08.000
He was a keen student who loved to read and
developed an early interest in philosophy.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.360
By the age of ten, though, he had decided
that he was going to pursue a life in the

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.360
military.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.569
From that point onward, Charles was single-minded
in his military focus.

00:04:16.569 --> 00:04:20.389
At the age of fifteen, he wrote an essay in
which he projected himself forward to the

00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:26.160
year 1930 where, as General De Gaulle, he
led the French army in defeating the Germans,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.410
finally inflicting vengeance for the defeat
of 1870.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:33.210
Charles was determined to win a place at the
military academy at Saint Cyr.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:37.430
His hard work at school paid off and he was
accepted into the Academy in 1909.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:41.569
However, prospective officers were required
to serve a year in the ranks before entering

00:04:41.569 --> 00:04:46.521
officer training school and so, in October
1909, he was enlisted into the 33rd Infantry

00:04:46.521 --> 00:04:48.939
Regiment stationed at Arras.

00:04:48.939 --> 00:04:51.620
Four months later he won his first promotion,
to corporal.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.819
It was usual for an aspiring officer to reach
the rank of sergeant during that first year,

00:04:55.819 --> 00:05:00.639
but De Gaulle’s company commander, Captain
Tuguy, refused to bestow this rank upon him.

00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:03.150
When asked why he had not promoted De Gaulle,
Tuguy commented . . .

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:08.150
Would you have me nominate to sergeant a boy
who would only be at his ease as the grand

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:09.150
constable?

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.360
In reflecting upon this time with the 33rd
Infantry, De Gaulle later made the wry remark

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.669
that the most valuable lesson he had gotten
from his year in the ranks was that if you

00:05:17.669 --> 00:05:21.979
did the exact opposite to what you were told
by the non-commissioned officers, you would

00:05:21.979 --> 00:05:24.229
get on fairly well.

00:05:24.229 --> 00:05:31.770
La Grande Asperge
He entered the Military Academy of St. Cyr

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.620
in the Autumn of 1910.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:37.840
He quickly earned a reputation as an arrogant
dandy with an air of superiority.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.800
Two nicknames quickly became attached to him
– ‘Le Grande Charles’ and ‘La Grande

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:43.800
Asperge’.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.870
This last title translates as ‘the great
asparagus’ and was, apparently a reference

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:49.219
to his stature.

00:05:49.219 --> 00:05:53.180
At 6 foot, five inches in height he towered
over virtually everyone he met.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:58.729
One of his classmates was the future General
Bethouart, commander of French troops in 1940’s

00:05:58.729 --> 00:05:59.729
Battle of Norway.

00:05:59.729 --> 00:06:03.440
He remembered De Gaulle as a mediocre student
who had few friends.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.599
The fact that he graduated 13th out of a class
of 211 students in 1912 seems to indicate

00:06:08.599 --> 00:06:10.830
that he was somewhat more than mediocre.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:15.270
It was expected that a young man with the
ambition of De Gaulle would choose to join

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:19.940
a cavalry regiment as that was traditionally
the fastest route to promotion through the

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:20.940
ranks.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:25.310
However, Charles had already come to the conclusion
that the days of the horse soldier were over.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.689
He believed that future wars would be won
by the infantry.

00:06:28.689 --> 00:06:32.819
As a result, he made the decision to return
to the 33rd Infantry at Arras.

00:06:32.819 --> 00:06:36.309
De Gaulle spent the next two years learning
the craft of an army officer.

00:06:36.309 --> 00:06:40.470
It didn’t take long for the lanky officer
to make an impression in the town of Arras.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:44.690
Heads turned when he passed by, with people
being captivated by both his extraordinary

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:46.589
height and his regal bearing.

00:06:46.589 --> 00:06:52.639
Yet, his rather caustic, cold nature repelled
more than his impressive appearance attracted.

00:06:52.639 --> 00:06:57.180
Not long after De Gaulle’s arrival at Arras,
the 33rd received a new commanding officer,

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:58.580
Colonel Phillipe Pétain.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.180
Charles developed a great deal of respect
for Petain.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:05.240
In his memoirs he wrote . . .
My first colonel, Pétain, taught me the art

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:06.240
of command.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.509
Petain was also impressed with De Gaulle.

00:07:08.509 --> 00:07:14.189
On October, 1913, he recommended his promotion
to First Lieutenant.

00:07:14.189 --> 00:07:22.389
First World War
When war broke out in August, 1914, the 33rd

00:07:22.389 --> 00:07:27.539
Infantry Regiment, as part of the French Fifth
Army was deployed to Dinant in order to halt

00:07:27.539 --> 00:07:29.589
the German advance throughout that region.

00:07:29.589 --> 00:07:33.930
De Gaulle was immediately into the action,
with his first engagement on August 15th being

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:35.089
the Battle of Dinant.

00:07:35.089 --> 00:07:38.319
He was wounded in the knee and sent to hospital
to recover.

00:07:38.319 --> 00:07:42.899
When he returned to the regiment in October,
many of his men and fellow officers were dead.

00:07:42.899 --> 00:07:46.159
Two months later, he was promoted to regimental
adjutant.

00:07:46.159 --> 00:07:50.819
The 33rd infantry regiment became famous for
their ability to crawl out into no-man’s

00:07:50.819 --> 00:07:52.659
land in order to spy on the enemy.

00:07:52.659 --> 00:07:57.009
The intelligence garnered from this work won
De Gaulle the Croix de Guerre medal.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:59.719
In February, 1915, he was promoted to captain.

00:07:59.719 --> 00:08:03.990
The following month an inconsequential hand
wound became infected, leaving him out of

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:06.069
action for the next four months.

00:08:06.069 --> 00:08:10.460
During the Battle of Verdun in March, 1916,
De Gaulle was wounded in the left thigh by

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:15.289
a bayonet and subsequently captured after
passing out from the effects of poison gas.

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:18.919
De Gaulle spent the remainder of the war in
a German prisoner of war camp.

00:08:18.919 --> 00:08:20.889
He attempted to escape five times.

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:24.509
He attempted escape by hiding in a laundry
basket, digging a tunnel, digging a hole through

00:08:24.509 --> 00:08:28.169
a wall, and even posing as a nurse to fool
his guards.

00:08:28.169 --> 00:08:32.320
In letters that he wrote to his parents during
his captivity he expressed his great frustration

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.250
that he was unable to participate in the war
effort any longer.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:40.089
He referred to himself as being ‘cuckolded’
and fell into a state of depression.

00:08:40.089 --> 00:08:44.730
Following the Armistice in 1918, he was released
and returned to his parents’ home in Dordogne

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:45.730
in southern France.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:55.180
The Inter War Years
In early 1920, De Gaulle was posted to Poland

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:57.540
as part of the French Military Mission to
Poland.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.850
There he served as an instructor to the Polish
Infantry.

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:05.000
He served with distinction, earning the rank
of major in the Polish army and being awarded

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:09.730
the distinguished Virtuti Militari, Poland’s
highest military decoration.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:14.050
He returned to France in 1921 and spent a
year as a military history lecturer at the

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:15.180
St. Cyr academy.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:19.430
He then spent two years studying at the Ecole
Militaire in Paris.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:23.579
He graduated with a grade of assez bien, or
‘good enough’, and was then posted to

00:09:23.579 --> 00:09:27.340
the Rhineland area of Germany which was under
French Army occupation.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:31.940
During the mid-1920’s De Gaulle worked as
a ghost-writer for his old commander at Arras,

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:33.610
the now Marshal Pétain.

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:37.780
In 1926, the two had a falling out over a
book on the history of the French soldier

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:40.940
that De Gaulle had ghost-written called Le
Soldat.

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:45.120
In October of that year he returned to the
Rhine to commence his military duties there.

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:50.190
In 1927, after a dozen years in the army,
De Gaulle was promoted to commandant.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:54.860
He was then posted to the occupying forces
at Trier in Germany at the head of the 19th

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:58.279
chasseurs a pied, which was a light infantry
battalion.

00:09:58.279 --> 00:10:03.100
De Gaulle was a hard taskmaster on his soldiers,
often pushing them beyond what was reasonable.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:06.610
On one occasion he got into hot water for
throwing a soldier into prison for exercising

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:11.190
his right to appeal to his member of parliament
for a transfer to a less demanding unit.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:14.600
Even though he had had a falling out with
Pétain, he called on his old commander to

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:16.160
help get himself out of the mess.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:20.440
With the end of the Allied occupation of the
Rhineland in 1929, De Gaulle was posted to

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:21.839
Lebanon and Syria.

00:10:21.839 --> 00:10:26.290
He served with distinction, with his commanding
officer providing him with an impressive recommendation

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:29.019
when he returned to France in 1931.

00:10:29.019 --> 00:10:33.540
He then received a posting to the General
Secretariat of the Supreme War Council, based

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:34.540
in Paris.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:37.180
His job description was as a drafting officer.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.880
For the next six years, De Gaulle gained valuable
experience in military bureaucracy.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:45.519
Over that time, he worked with government
officials in drafting bills related to the

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:46.519
military.

00:10:46.519 --> 00:10:49.510
He worked on a bill for the organization of
France during the event of war, but it did

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:51.110
not pass the senate.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:55.420
Throughout this period, De Gaulle was not
shy about voicing his opinion on the military

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:59.300
preparations that France was making in the
event of another European war.

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:03.430
He considered the line of fortifications known
as the Maginot Line, on which billions of

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:06.290
dollars was being spent, to be a wasted effort.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:10.550
Rather than focusing on a defensive footing,
he contended that the French needed to be

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:14.130
pro-active through mechanized warfare that
was fast moving.

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:17.890
In 1934, he wrote a book called ‘Toward
A Professional Army.’

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:22.000
In it he outlined his plans for the Future
French Army, with a hundred thousand men and

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:23.670
three thousand tanks.

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:28.510
In 1937, De Gaulle came a step closer to realizing
this ambition when he was given command of

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:30.600
the 507th Tank Regiment.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.350
With his promotion of the tank as the key
to French infantry success, he became a nationally

00:11:35.350 --> 00:12:42.230
known figure, with people referring to him
as ‘Colonel Motors.’

00:12:42.230 --> 00:13:02.730
France Falls
With the French declaration of war upon Germany

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:07.889
in September, 1939, De Gaulle was put in command
of the Fifth Army’s five tank battalions,

00:13:07.889 --> 00:13:09.699
mostly equipped with R35 light tanks.

00:13:09.699 --> 00:13:14.899
In early 1940, he lobbied for the position
of Secretary General of the War Council, the

00:13:14.899 --> 00:13:18.660
government’s top military adviser, but was
passed over.

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:23.560
Towards the end of March 1940, De Gaulle was
given command of the newly created 4th Armoured

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:24.560
Division.

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:27.910
With the German attack on May 10th, the 4th
Armoured went into action.

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:31.040
On May 17th, they encountered the Germans
at Montcornet, near Laon.

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:36.420
It was a disaster for De Gaulle, with 23 of
90 of his tanks destroyed by mines, anti-tank

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:39.610
weapons, or Stukas, dive bomber and ground-attack
aircraft.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:43.680
Two days later his reinforced division was
decimated for a second time.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:48.380
Despite being ordered to withdraw, he fought
on, demanding even more reinforcements.

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:51.939
Even though this request was denied he managed
to push the Germans back to Caumont.

00:13:51.939 --> 00:13:53.800
But this was only a temporary respite.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:58.509
On May 20th, De Gaulle began to retreat in
the face of the unstoppable German advance.

00:13:58.509 --> 00:14:01.449
On May 23rd he was promoted to brigadier-general.

00:14:01.449 --> 00:14:07.420
On May 28th he led an attack on a German bridgehead
at Abbeville, which captured some 400 prisoners.

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:10.970
By the beginning of June, De Gaulle had been
recalled to Paris where he was offered and

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:14.730
accepted the position of under-secretary of
state for National Defence for War.

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:19.129
On June 8th, he met with Army Commander in
Chief Weygand who was about to announce the

00:14:19.129 --> 00:14:20.129
French surrender.

00:14:20.129 --> 00:14:24.379
When De Gaulle tried to convince him to fight
on, Weygand laughed despairingly.

00:14:24.379 --> 00:14:27.779
The following day De Gaulle flew to London
where he met British Prime Minister Winston

00:14:27.779 --> 00:14:29.260
Churchill for the first time.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:33.589
His attempts to convince Churchill to put
more RAF fighters into the Battle for France

00:14:33.589 --> 00:14:35.220
fell on deaf ears.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:39.269
Upon his return to Paris, he wanted to defend
the city to the last man, but Weygand had

00:14:39.269 --> 00:14:40.269
other ideas.

00:14:40.269 --> 00:14:44.199
The government was relocated to Tours, where
a series of meetings with British officials

00:14:44.199 --> 00:14:45.199
were held.

00:14:45.199 --> 00:14:48.940
During these meetings, De Gaulle’s fighting
resolve impressed the Brits, standing in stark

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:51.220
contrast with the other French leaders.

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:55.110
On June 16th, De Gaulle was back in London
discussing the logistics of a troop withdrawal

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:56.779
to French North Africa.

00:14:56.779 --> 00:14:59.610
When he returned to France later that day,
he discovered that Prime Minister Reynaud

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:04.310
had resigned to be replaced by Marshal Pétain,
who was intent on signing an armistice with

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:05.310
the Germans.

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:08.660
De Gaulle now fled France for London ahead
of the German take-over.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:13.120
On June 18th, Churchill offered him air time
on BBC radio to address the French people.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:17.389
In the speech, De Gaulle encouraged his countrymen
to be brave during the occupation, resisting

00:15:17.389 --> 00:15:18.879
it as best they could.

00:15:18.879 --> 00:15:20.779
The next day he gave another broadcast.

00:15:20.779 --> 00:15:25.290
This time he was more critical of the Pétain
government, denouncing it as illegitimate.

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:29.790
On June 22nd, following the signing of the
armistice, De Gaulle took to the BBC again.

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:33.879
He denounced the surrender and again declared
the French government illegitimate.

00:15:33.879 --> 00:15:38.269
On June 28th, the British government recognized
De Gaulle as the leader of Free France.

00:15:38.269 --> 00:15:42.920
His government in exile consisted of three
colonels, twelve captains and three battalions

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:44.149
of legionnaires.

00:15:44.149 --> 00:15:47.149
Over the following months, De Gaulle’s main
weapon was the BBC radio.

00:15:47.149 --> 00:15:51.189
He would give broadcasts an average of three
times per month, denouncing the occupation

00:15:51.189 --> 00:15:55.230
government based in Vichy and urging Frenchmen
to resist the occupation.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:57.870
The Pétain government sentenced De Gaulle
to death in absentia.

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:02.009
Throughout the war years, De Gaulle was able
to slowly build up the Free French Army.

00:16:02.009 --> 00:16:06.850
On 21st April, 1943 he boarded a plane en
route to Scotland to inspect his navy.

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:10.870
The plane almost crashed on take-off and it
was later found that it had been sabotaged

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:11.990
using acid.

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:16.910
In May 1943, De Gaulle moved his headquarters
to French Algiers, where he became the head

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:19.120
of the French Committee of National Liberation.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:22.860
He became involved in the planning for the
D-Day, invasion, although the Americans and

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:27.230
British became increasingly frustrated with
his insistence that he was the rightful leader

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:28.230
of France.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:32.540
He returned to Britain on June 4th, 1944 whereupon
Churchill asked him to address the French

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:36.290
people over BBC radio in anticipation of the
allied invasion.

00:16:36.290 --> 00:16:40.800
However, the script he was given did not acknowledge
him as the legitimate interim ruler of France

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:44.589
and so he refused to deliver it.

00:16:44.589 --> 00:16:50.790
Reclaiming France
In the wake of the D-Day invasion of June

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:56.029
6th, 1944 the Free French Army, under the
leadership of General de Lattre Tassigny,

00:16:56.029 --> 00:17:00.069
landed in southern France and were instrumental
in pushing back the Germans.

00:17:00.069 --> 00:17:02.569
On June 14th, De Gaulle returned to France.

00:17:02.569 --> 00:17:06.620
Travelling through newly liberated Norman
towns, he was well received by the townspeople.

00:17:06.620 --> 00:17:11.270
He established the capital of Free France
in Bayeux and then set off to Rome where he

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:13.670
met with the newly installed Italian government.

00:17:13.670 --> 00:17:17.240
From there he flew to Washington for his first
meeting with President Roosevelt.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:21.809
The visit was a strained one, with Roosevelt
not providing the usual privileges of a visiting

00:17:21.809 --> 00:17:22.939
head of state.

00:17:22.939 --> 00:17:27.630
De Gaulle pushed for the allies to prioritize
the liberation of Paris, fearing a communist

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:28.630
take-over.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:32.210
But there was no strategic priority for the
allies to focus on Paris.

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:36.320
Still, through De Gaulle’s insistence, Supreme
Allied Commander Dwight Eisenhower agreed

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:40.210
that Paris would be prioritized out of humanitarian
and symbolic need.

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:44.180
He also permitted the Free French Army to
be the first to enter the French capital.

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:49.100
On Saturday, August 26th, De Gaulle entered
Paris in a triumphal march down the Champs

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.100
Elysees.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:53.310
As his procession made its way toward the
Notre Dame Cathedral, it came under machine

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:55.230
gun fire from Vichy government militia.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:18:00.150
A BBC reporter who was present noted De Gaulle’s
demeanor as he confidently strode forward

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:03.030
. . .
General de Gaulle walked straight ahead into

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:07.530
what appeared to me to be a hail of fire ... but
he went straight ahead without hesitation,

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:12.140
his shoulders flung back, and walked right
down the centre aisle, even while the bullets

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:13.330
were pouring about him.

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:17.130
It was the most extraordinary example of courage
I have ever seen.

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:21.760
Upon reaching the Great Hall of Hotel de Ville,
De Gaulle spoke before an ecstatic crowd.

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:24.060
His inspiring message included these words
. . .

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:25.490
Paris!

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:29.640
Paris outraged, Paris broken, Paris martyred,
but Paris liberated!

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:33.840
Liberated by itself, liberated by its people
with the assistance of the armies of France,

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:34.840
wit De Gaulle commuted around a thousand of
them.

00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:36.830
h the support and assistance of the whole
of France!

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:40.030
... The enemy is faltering but he is not yet
beaten.

00:18:40.030 --> 00:18:41.920
He is still on our soil.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:46.760
It will not suffice that we, with the assistance
of our dear and admirable allies, will have

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:51.430
chased him from our home in order to be satisfied
after what has happened.

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:54.760
We want to enter his territory, as is fitting,
as conquerors.

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:58.530
... It is for this revenge, this vengeance
and this justice, that we will continue to

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:04.160
fight until the last day, until the day of
the total and complete victory.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:08.370
With his bold and triumphant entry into Paris,
De Gaulle won the respect of the world.

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:12.110
In the days that followed the allied leaders
made statements recognizing his government

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:13.880
as the legitimate ruler of France.

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:18.230
The provisional Government of the French Republic
was established on September 10th, 1944.

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:21.320
De Gaulle then set out on a tour of the country.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:26.180
In each city he freely mixed with the crowds
with no regard for his own safety.

00:19:26.180 --> 00:19:30.750
Towards the end of 1944, a Legal Purge was
established to punish traitors during the

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:34.710
occupation and remove the last vestiges of
the Vichy Government.

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:40.760
Over the course of the purge around 2,000
people were sentenced to death.In May, 1945

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:46.510
the Germans surrendered, signing an armistice
with France in Berlin.

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:51.410
However, on the very day that victory in Europe
was proclaimed, May 8th, riots broke out in

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:52.630
French Tunisia.

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:56.960
Twelve days later, the French artillery fired
on demonstrators in Damascus, leaving hundreds

00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:57.960
dead.

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:02.050
This event caused the ever testy relationship
between De Gaulle and Churchill to reach a

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:07.140
low ebb. Churchill commented that De Gaulle
was ‘a great danger for peace and to Great

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:13.990
Britain.’

00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:15.960
The New France
The French Constituent assembly unanimously

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:20.740
elected De Gaulle as head of the French government
on November 13th, 1945.

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:24.830
After two months of struggle with the Communists
within his government he abruptly resigned

00:20:24.830 --> 00:20:27.940
and formed the right-wing Rally of the French
People.

00:20:27.940 --> 00:20:32.320
He believed that his popularity as a war hero
would propel him back to power, but this gamble

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:33.679
failed to pay off.

00:20:33.679 --> 00:20:37.371
After three years in the political wilderness,
he left the party and politics behind.

00:20:37.371 --> 00:20:46.270
The Algerian Crisis
De Gaulle then retired from public life to

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:47.270
write his memoirs.

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:50.970
In 1958, a crisis in Algeria brought him back
to politics.

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:55.330
The Algerian National Liberation Front was
waging a war for independence and De Gaulle

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:58.520
was seen as the kind of strong national leader
who could quash it.

00:20:58.520 --> 00:21:03.370
He was returned to the Presidency and proceeded
to use brutal force to put down the rebels.

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:06.150
Bu these harsh methods backfired.

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:10.251
The Algerian people were driven further towards
independence, while around the world’s there

00:21:10.251 --> 00:21:12.760
was condemnation against the French government.

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:17.220
Under intense pressure from within his country
and abroad, De Gaulle finally granted independence

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:20.130
to all thirteen French African colonies in
1962.

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:25.440
As the Cold War developed, De Gaulle advocated
the development of a French nuclear arsenal.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:29.130
The country became the world’s fourth nuclear
power in February, 1960.

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:32.980
Three years later, De Gaulle refused to sign
the Partial Test Ban Treaty.

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:37.250
In 1965, he was re-elected for a second seven-year
term as French President.

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:41.630
He ran his government and his country with
a heavy-handed, dogmatic style that often

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:42.690
drew criticism.

00:21:42.690 --> 00:21:47.170
In early, 1969 he called for a nationwide
referendum on the reform of the senate.

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:50.820
When his proposal was rejected by the people,
he offered his resignation, following through

00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:56.750
on a televised promise made two days before
the referendum.

00:21:56.750 --> 00:22:02.800
End of the Line
At the age of 78, having lived an extraordinarily

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:06.650
full public life, De Gaulle retired to write
an updated version of his memoirs.

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:10.680
He died suddenly on November 9th, 1970 while
watching TV.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:14.540
He complained to his wife that he was feeling
pain in his neck and then collapsed to the

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:15.540
ground.

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:16.730
He died within minutes.

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:19.900
The autopsy revealed that he had suffered
from a ruptured blood vessel.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:25.160
The funeral of Charles De Gaulle on November
12th, 19070 was the largest in French history.

00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:42.730
Today he ranks along Napoleon as among the
greatest of French leaders.

